1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:02,446
A neural network is a stack of layers.

2
00:00:02,872 --> 00:00:05,731
Each layer multiplies its input by a matrix.

3
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:07,513
So here is a strange fact.

4
00:00:07,925 --> 00:00:14,760
If that is all the layers do, then a network with one hundred layers is no smarter than a network with one layer.

5
00:00:15,172 --> 00:00:16,941
All that depth is wasted.

6
00:00:17,380 --> 00:00:21,529
In this video we find out why, and we fix it with one small bend.

7
00:00:22,439 --> 00:00:24,953
That bend is called an activation function.

8
00:00:25,391 --> 00:00:33,224
You will see why two linear layers collapse into one, how the ReLU function works, and how a few bends can draw a curve.

9
00:00:33,649 --> 00:00:35,697
This is the last atom of the series.

10
00:00:36,123 --> 00:00:41,335
You need matrix multiplication from video two, and the derivative from video seven.

11
00:00:43,073 --> 00:00:45,440
Let's test the claim with real numbers.

12
00:00:45,865 --> 00:00:49,708
Here is a toy input x, equal to three, minus one.

13
00:00:50,147 --> 00:00:56,038
The first layer is the matrix W one, with rows one, two and zero, one.

14
00:00:56,477 --> 00:01:01,410
Multiply: one times three plus two times minus one is one.

15
00:01:01,836 --> 00:01:05,998
Zero times three plus one times minus one is minus one.

16
00:01:06,424 --> 00:01:08,977
So the first layer gives one, minus one.

17
00:01:09,906 --> 00:01:16,089
The second layer is the matrix W two, with rows two, zero and one, one.

18
00:01:16,528 --> 00:01:19,068
Multiply the result one, minus one.

19
00:01:19,507 --> 00:01:21,980
Two times one plus zero is two.

20
00:01:22,406 --> 00:01:26,010
One times one plus one times minus one is zero.

21
00:01:26,435 --> 00:01:29,680
So two layers in a row give two, zero.

22
00:01:30,606 --> 00:01:33,465
Now multiply the two matrices first.

23
00:01:33,877 --> 00:01:40,406
W two times W one is the matrix with rows two, four and one, three.

24
00:01:40,832 --> 00:01:42,813
Apply this one matrix to x.

25
00:01:43,239 --> 00:01:46,776
Two times three plus four times minus one is two.

26
00:01:47,215 --> 00:01:51,218
One times three plus three times minus one is zero.

27
00:01:51,643 --> 00:01:53,146
Again two, zero.

28
00:01:53,598 --> 00:01:56,231
One matrix did the job of two layers.

29
00:01:57,773 --> 00:01:58,663
This is not luck.

30
00:01:59,089 --> 00:02:07,945
Matrix multiplication is associative: W two times W one x equals W two W one, times x.

31
00:02:08,358 --> 00:02:12,919
So any stack of linear layers is just one linear layer in disguise.

32
00:02:13,331 --> 00:02:17,959
Linear maps can only stretch, turn, flip and squash space.

33
00:02:18,398 --> 00:02:20,618
Stacking them never creates a curve.

34
00:02:22,353 --> 00:02:26,196
The fix is to put something that is not linear between the layers.

35
00:02:26,608 --> 00:02:30,198
A function that is not linear is called a nonlinearity.

36
00:02:30,597 --> 00:02:36,821
In a neural network, the nonlinearity applied after a layer is called an activation function.

37
00:02:37,246 --> 00:02:39,174
It acts on every number separately.

38
00:02:40,706 --> 00:02:45,998
The simplest and most popular one is ReLU, the rectified linear unit.

39
00:02:46,411 --> 00:02:50,147
ReLU of z is the maximum of zero and z.

40
00:02:50,573 --> 00:02:53,432
In words: if z is positive, keep it.

41
00:02:53,871 --> 00:02:57,049
If z is negative, replace it with zero.

42
00:02:57,488 --> 00:03:02,541
The graph is flat at zero on the left, and a straight diagonal line on the right.

43
00:03:03,473 --> 00:03:04,856
Let's compute some values.

44
00:03:05,281 --> 00:03:07,701
ReLU of minus two is zero.

45
00:03:08,114 --> 00:03:11,571
ReLU of minus zero point five is also zero.

46
00:03:11,997 --> 00:03:13,898
ReLU of zero is zero.

47
00:03:14,310 --> 00:03:17,462
ReLU of one point five is one point five.

48
00:03:17,901 --> 00:03:20,068
And ReLU of three is three.

49
00:03:20,494 --> 00:03:22,276
Negative numbers are switched off.

50
00:03:22,728 --> 00:03:25,241
Positive numbers pass through unchanged.

51
00:03:26,773 --> 00:03:29,472
Now put ReLU between our two layers.

52
00:03:29,911 --> 00:03:32,171
The first layer gave one, minus one.

53
00:03:32,570 --> 00:03:35,443
ReLU turns that into one, zero.

54
00:03:35,882 --> 00:03:38,288
Then W two gives two, one.

55
00:03:38,727 --> 00:03:41,148
Without ReLU we got two, zero.

56
00:03:41,586 --> 00:03:46,733
The answer changed, so the two layers no longer collapse into one matrix.

57
00:03:47,158 --> 00:03:48,913
The bend broke the shortcut.

58
00:03:50,659 --> 00:03:52,401
ReLU is not the only bend.

59
00:03:52,827 --> 00:03:57,321
The sigmoid function squashes any number into the range from zero to one.

60
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:01,750
It is smooth, and sigmoid of zero is exactly one half.

61
00:04:02,202 --> 00:04:05,500
That makes it useful when the output should look like a probability.

62
00:04:06,439 --> 00:04:13,593
The tanh function, the hyperbolic tangent, has a similar S shape, but its range is from minus one to one.

63
00:04:14,019 --> 00:04:15,827
Tanh of zero is zero.

64
00:04:16,266 --> 00:04:17,543
It is smooth as well.

65
00:04:17,968 --> 00:04:24,445
Both sigmoid and tanh become very flat for large positive or large negative inputs.

66
00:04:25,359 --> 00:04:27,633
GELU is a smooth version of ReLU.

67
00:04:28,045 --> 00:04:33,990
It multiplies z by the probability that a standard normal random value is below z.

68
00:04:34,455 --> 00:04:37,088
For large positive z it is almost z.

69
00:04:37,567 --> 00:04:40,412
For large negative z it is almost zero.

70
00:04:40,838 --> 00:04:43,524
GELU of one is about zero point eight four.

71
00:04:44,426 --> 00:04:45,849
Look closely at the left side.

72
00:04:46,274 --> 00:04:48,828
GELU dips slightly below zero.

73
00:04:49,266 --> 00:04:53,296
GELU of minus one is about minus zero point one six.

74
00:04:53,708 --> 00:04:56,660
So negative inputs still give a small output.

75
00:04:57,085 --> 00:05:01,726
GPT-2 uses GELU inside every transformer block.

76
00:05:03,473 --> 00:05:06,132
How can simple bends build complex shapes?

77
00:05:06,571 --> 00:05:11,598
Let's try to draw the curve y equals x squared, between minus two and two.

78
00:05:12,023 --> 00:05:14,204
We will use only ReLU pieces.

79
00:05:14,616 --> 00:05:17,994
Each piece is a shifted ReLU, times a weight.

80
00:05:18,419 --> 00:05:20,986
Each piece adds one corner to the graph.

81
00:05:21,926 --> 00:05:25,942
Start with the constant four, the height of the curve at minus two.

82
00:05:26,367 --> 00:05:29,519
Add minus three times ReLU of x plus two.

83
00:05:29,945 --> 00:05:32,538
Now the line goes down with slope minus three.

84
00:05:32,976 --> 00:05:35,317
Add two times ReLU of x plus one.

85
00:05:35,729 --> 00:05:39,785
At minus one, the slope changes from minus three to minus one.

86
00:05:40,706 --> 00:05:43,126
Add two times ReLU of x.

87
00:05:43,552 --> 00:05:46,344
At zero, the slope becomes plus one.

88
00:05:46,783 --> 00:05:49,602
Add two times ReLU of x minus one.

89
00:05:50,041 --> 00:05:52,674
At one, the slope becomes plus three.

90
00:05:53,126 --> 00:05:59,177
Four pieces, and we have a bent line that touches the curve at every whole number from minus two to two.

91
00:06:00,106 --> 00:06:01,010
How close is it?

92
00:06:01,409 --> 00:06:05,518
Our demo measured the largest gap between the bent line and the curve.

93
00:06:05,944 --> 00:06:08,922
With four pieces the gap is zero point two five.

94
00:06:09,348 --> 00:06:13,311
With eight pieces it is only zero point zero six two five.

95
00:06:13,736 --> 00:06:15,678
More pieces, smaller error.

96
00:06:16,117 --> 00:06:23,377
With enough ReLU units, a network can approximate any continuous curve on an interval as closely as you like.

97
00:06:25,126 --> 00:06:28,051
Training needs slopes, as we saw in video fifteen.

98
00:06:28,490 --> 00:06:30,605
So what is the derivative of ReLU?

99
00:06:31,017 --> 00:06:34,115
On the right side the graph is a line with slope one.

100
00:06:34,528 --> 00:06:38,224
On the left side it is flat, so the slope is zero.

101
00:06:38,650 --> 00:06:40,631
At exactly zero there is a corner.

102
00:06:41,043 --> 00:06:44,275
Libraries such as PyTorch simply use zero there.

103
00:06:45,206 --> 00:06:47,028
Our demo checked this numerically.

104
00:06:47,453 --> 00:06:49,967
At minus two the slope is zero.

105
00:06:50,405 --> 00:06:52,267
At three the slope is one.

106
00:06:52,693 --> 00:06:58,052
A slope of one is good news: the signal passes back through the unit without shrinking.

107
00:06:58,477 --> 00:07:05,472
This is one reason ReLU replaced sigmoid and tanh, whose slopes become tiny in their flat tails.

108
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:09,054
But a slope of zero has a dark side.

109
00:07:09,479 --> 00:07:15,570
Imagine a unit with weight one and bias minus ten, and inputs between zero and five.

110
00:07:15,982 --> 00:07:18,801
Its input to ReLU is always negative.

111
00:07:19,213 --> 00:07:24,293
So its output is always zero, and its slope is always zero.

112
00:07:24,719 --> 00:07:28,894
The demo found zero inputs, out of eleven, with slope one.

113
00:07:29,839 --> 00:07:33,563
Such a unit gets no learning signal, so it never changes.

114
00:07:34,001 --> 00:07:35,557
It is called a dead ReLU.

115
00:07:35,983 --> 00:07:39,028
This is one reason for smooth cousins like GELU.

116
00:07:39,454 --> 00:07:46,049
Their slope for negative inputs is small, but it is not exactly zero, so the unit can still learn.

117
00:07:47,773 --> 00:07:49,980
Now let's see where activations live.

118
00:07:50,379 --> 00:07:59,714
A multilayer perceptron, or MLP, alternates layers and bends: linear, ReLU, linear, ReLU, linear.

119
00:08:00,126 --> 00:08:07,227
The book Build a Large Language Model uses exactly this pattern, with thirty and then twenty hidden units.

120
00:08:07,666 --> 00:08:12,320
There is one activation after every linear layer, except the last.

121
00:08:13,226 --> 00:08:21,138
In a transformer like GPT-2, each block has a small feed-forward network: linear, GELU, linear.

122
00:08:21,564 --> 00:08:25,207
The GELU is the only nonlinear piece in that sub-network.

123
00:08:25,606 --> 00:08:29,502
An activation like ReLU or GELU has no parameters at all.

124
00:08:29,928 --> 00:08:34,263
It adds zero to the parameter count, but it is what makes depth useful.

125
00:08:35,773 --> 00:08:37,023
Where does this atom come from?

126
00:08:37,461 --> 00:08:40,400
The layers come from axiom A, linearity.

127
00:08:40,812 --> 00:08:45,081
The activation is the one piece that is deliberately not linear.

128
00:08:45,493 --> 00:08:48,140
Linearity alone gives one matrix.

129
00:08:48,552 --> 00:08:51,664
Linearity plus a bend gives deep learning.

130
00:08:53,373 --> 00:08:54,237
Now it is your turn.

131
00:08:54,662 --> 00:08:56,404
Pause the video and try this.

132
00:08:56,843 --> 00:09:01,484
Apply ReLU to the vector minus three, zero point five, two.

133
00:09:01,923 --> 00:09:05,487
Then give the slope of ReLU at each of the three entries.

134
00:09:05,912 --> 00:09:06,551
Take a moment.

135
00:09:07,486 --> 00:09:08,310
Here is the answer.

136
00:09:08,723 --> 00:09:11,090
ReLU of minus three is zero.

137
00:09:11,502 --> 00:09:14,680
ReLU of zero point five is zero point five.

138
00:09:15,106 --> 00:09:16,887
ReLU of two is two.

139
00:09:17,326 --> 00:09:20,824
So the result is zero, zero point five, two.

140
00:09:21,262 --> 00:09:27,007
The slopes are zero, one and one, because only the first entry is negative.

141
00:09:28,539 --> 00:09:29,324
One more question.

142
00:09:29,749 --> 00:09:35,042
Define g of x as ReLU of x plus ReLU of minus x.

143
00:09:35,467 --> 00:09:36,864
What is g of minus two?

144
00:09:37,276 --> 00:09:38,473
What is g of three?

145
00:09:38,898 --> 00:09:40,241
Can you name the function g?

146
00:09:40,667 --> 00:09:41,345
Pause and try.

147
00:09:42,346 --> 00:09:48,782
For minus two: ReLU of minus two is zero, and ReLU of two is two.

148
00:09:49,207 --> 00:09:51,309
So g of minus two is two.

149
00:09:51,774 --> 00:09:58,396
For three: ReLU of three is three, and ReLU of minus three is zero.

150
00:09:58,835 --> 00:10:00,843
So g of three is three.

151
00:10:01,269 --> 00:10:03,596
The function g is the absolute value.

152
00:10:04,035 --> 00:10:06,628
Two ReLU pieces made a corner at zero.

153
00:10:08,339 --> 00:10:11,198
Let's check everything in Python with NumPy.

154
00:10:11,624 --> 00:10:15,746
ReLU is one line: the maximum of zero and z.

155
00:10:16,158 --> 00:10:21,571
We compare two layers with one combined matrix, and then with ReLU in between.

156
00:10:22,009 --> 00:10:26,730
Then we build the curve x squared from shifted ReLUs and measure the error.

157
00:10:27,222 --> 00:10:29,044
Finally, we check the slopes.

158
00:10:30,573 --> 00:10:34,402
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

159
00:10:34,828 --> 00:10:38,830
Two layers and one matrix both give two, zero.

160
00:10:39,269 --> 00:10:41,503
With ReLU in between we get two, one.

161
00:10:41,929 --> 00:10:49,722
The four ReLU weights are minus three, two, two and two, with a largest error of zero point two five.

162
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:55,652
The slopes are zero and one, and the dead unit has slope one for zero inputs.

163
00:10:57,406 --> 00:10:58,084
In summary.

164
00:10:58,496 --> 00:11:06,089
A stack of linear layers is just one linear layer, because matrix multiplication is associative.

165
00:11:06,488 --> 00:11:10,385
An activation function puts a nonlinearity between the layers.

166
00:11:10,784 --> 00:11:15,238
ReLU keeps positive numbers and turns negative numbers into zero.

167
00:11:15,677 --> 00:11:19,307
Sigmoid, tanh and GELU are smooth alternatives.

168
00:11:20,226 --> 00:11:23,072
Sums of shifted ReLUs can draw curves.

169
00:11:23,497 --> 00:11:29,348
The slope of ReLU is zero or one, and units that are always negative can die.

170
00:11:29,761 --> 00:11:34,867
MLPs use ReLU, and GPT-2 uses GELU.

171
00:11:35,306 --> 00:11:36,782
That completes the atoms.

172
00:11:37,221 --> 00:11:43,896
Next, the molecules map shows how these atoms combine into gradient descent, attention and more.

173
00:11:44,348 --> 00:11:45,412
Thank you for learning with me.

174
00:11:47,339 --> 00:11:49,161
This video was made by David R.

175
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:52,831
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

176
00:11:53,390 --> 00:11:54,108
Thanks for watching.
